{"reference":"Malachi 3","book":"Malachi","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"language_literary","title":"A Chapter of Questions and Replies","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Malachi presents the chapter as a dispute: divine assertions are followed by the people’s questions, and then by extended replies. The call to “return” in verse 7 is immediately met with “How shall we return?” Later, the claim that serving God is futile is set beside those who fear the Lord and speak with one another. This pattern makes the chapter a sustained confrontation with the community’s objections, not simply a sequence of announcements.","verse_refs":["Malachi 3:1","Malachi 3:13-16","Malachi 3:7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_003_2d046be943cb0dee0087628a"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The Messenger in Verse 1","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Verse 1 distinguishes “my messenger,” “the Lord” whom the people seek, and “the messenger of the covenant.” The text itself does not securely identify these figures historically. The book’s title also leaves open whether malakhi is a personal name, a shortened name, or a title meaning “my messenger.”","verse_refs":["Malachi 3:1","Malachi 3:6","Malachi 3:6-18","Malachi 3:8-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_003_cfa8c08d24b5b067fb42fb5c"]},{"id":"block_3","text":"Mark 1:2–3 later uses Malachi 3:1 within a composite citation that also draws on Exodus and Isaiah, placing the whole citation under an Isaiah heading. This later use has prompted interpretive discussion about how the citation is arranged.","verse_refs":["Malachi 3:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_003_ad66e884040855de02927261"]}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Judgment and Community Life","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The judgment announced in verse 5 reaches beyond temple worship. It names sorcery, adultery, false testimony, withheld wages, and the mistreatment of widows, orphans, and resident aliens. The chapter’s concern with tithes and offerings belongs to a Persian-period setting marked by a functioning temple, priestly offerings, a storehouse, and an agrarian economy; those features should not simply be equated with later institutions or relationships.","verse_refs":["Malachi 3:5","Malachi 3:8-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_003_c5f48c998eaa2e78b782e4b2"]}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Purification, Provision, and Remembrance","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"The refiner’s fire and launderer’s alkali are images of cleansing: the sons of Levi are purified so that offerings may again be acceptable. The later charge of robbing God is specified as withholding tithes and offerings; the “whole tithe” is to be brought into the storehouse, with promised provision, protection from crop loss, and a fruitful land. Against the complaint that serving God brings no profit, the chapter also describes a book of remembrance for those who fear the Lord and esteem his name.","verse_refs":["Malachi 3:1-18","Malachi 3:10-12","Malachi 3:16","Malachi 3:2-4","Malachi 3:8-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_003_3f982856fbf95395daaf808f"]},{"id":"block_6","text":"The people who fear the Lord are promised the status of a treasured possession and compassion compared with that shown by a parent to a serving child. This promise leads into the chapter’s final contrast between the righteous and the wicked, and between those who serve God and those who do not.","verse_refs":["Malachi 3:17-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_malachi_003_d304b63c191534f7530ae76c"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}